WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.367
Let's now think about code efficiency.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:08.862
When people hear the word optimization, they sometimes immediately think about writing complicated algorithms

00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:11.232
or trying to make every line faster.

00:00:11.232 --> 00:00:14.252
That is usually not the best place to start.

00:00:14.252 --> 00:00:18.197
Python already provides many built-in functions that are clear, convenient, and efficient.

00:00:18.197 --> 00:00:21.722
For example, imagine that we want the total of a collection of numbers.

00:00:21.722 --> 00:00:27.552
We could create a variable called total, loop through every value, and add each number to total.

00:00:27.552 --> 00:00:32.297
That works. But Python already gives us the sum function.

00:00:32.297 --> 00:00:36.937
Similarly, max can find the largest value, min can find the smallest value,

00:00:36.937 --> 00:00:39.302
and len can tell us how many items we have.

00:00:39.302 --> 00:00:44.677
In the example, we create a range containing one million numbers and pass it directly to sum.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.267
Run the example and look at the result.

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:51.457
The important lesson is not just that this code is shorter.

00:00:51.457 --> 00:00:58.187
It is also easier for another Python programmer to immediately understand what we are trying to do.

00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:02.412
Whenever Python already provides a clear built-in operation, it is usually worth considering before writing a

00:01:02.412 --> 00:01:03.886
manual loop.
